# Admin HTTP crate guide

## Purpose

`<<[ package_name ]>>-admin-http` owns the privileged HTTP surface and its OpenAPI contract. It is deployed only through the separate `<<[ package_name ]>>-admin-api` binary and must never become a dependency of the public API binary.

## Key entrypoints

- `src/lib.rs`: privileged routes, the browser policy, `assemble`, `in_process`, and the admin OpenAPI document.
- `src/authorization.rs`: `AdminAuthorizer`, `DenyAllAdminAuthorizer`, and the authorization route layer.

## Edit here for X

- Add privileged handlers and DTOs to `routes()` with `#[utoipa::path]`; the boundary admits exactly the documented paths.
- Implement `AdminAuthorizer` for the real authentication/session mechanism, then replace `DenyAllAdminAuthorizer` in `apps/<<[ package_name ]>>-admin-api/src/main.rs`.
- Call application use cases through `AppState`; keep business logic in the owning application crate.

## Invariants

- Production entrypoints use `assemble` with the runtime's operation admission and readiness policy. `in_process` uses an independent, approved lifecycle for tests.
- The admin group uses `GroupPolicy::browser`: every response, including probes, method rejections, admission, deadline, and mutation rejections, carries `Cache-Control: no-store`, `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, and `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`.
- Mutating methods must carry `Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin` and the `x-admin-request` marker before authorization runs. The admin panel sends the marker; non-browser clients must send both headers. Keep the check if the authorizer accepts browser cookies, since it is the CSRF defense.
- Authorization runs inside admission and the request deadline; draining or not-yet-ready processes reject requests before authorization.
- Keep `/health/live` and `/health/ready` outside request admission and admin authorization; unauthenticated probes must not expose business data.
- Every matched privileged route passes through `AdminAuthorizer`; keep the generated deny-all authorizer until the real implementation and its tests are ready.
- Keep the authorizer fail closed on missing, malformed, expired, or unverifiable credentials. Use `Forbidden` only for an authenticated principal that lacks permission.
- Keep all privileged operations under `/admin-api` so public artifact checks can identify accidental bundling.
- Never include secrets, credentials, internal stack traces, SQL, or sensitive examples in OpenAPI descriptions.
- The public API binary and public frontend must not depend on this crate or its generated client.

## Common commands

- `cargo test -p <<[ package_name ]>>-admin-http`
- `cargo clippy -p <<[ package_name ]>>-admin-http --all-targets -- -D warnings`
